<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#partible-inheritance">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Partible inheritance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Partible inheritance is a custom by which a father's property is divided among all his sons rather than passing to a single heir. Under Frankish law it applied to kingdoms as well as estates, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> initially shared the realm with his brother Carloman. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> identify it as the structural cause of the empire's fragmentation after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>.</p></div>
